 "examples": [
  "To determine the speed \\( v \\) of the cart at the end of phase 1, we can use the kinematic equation that relates initial velocity, final velocity, acceleration, and distance covered. The relevant equation is:\n\n\\[ v^2 = u^2 + 2a_1s_1 \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( v \\) is the final velocity,\n- \\( u \\) is the initial velocity,\n- \\( a_1 \\) is the acceleration,\n- \\( s_1 \\) is the distance covered during phase 1.\n\nWe are given:\n- \\( u = 859.00 \\) cm/s,\n- \\( a_1 = 2.452 \\) m/s\\(^2\\),\n- \\( s_1 = 0.26465 \\) km.\n\nFirst, we need to convert \\( s_1 \\) from kilometers to meters:\n\\[ s_1 = 0.26465 \\text{ km} = 0.26465 \\times 1000 \\text{ m} = 264.65 \\text{ m} \\]\n\nNow, we can substitute the values into the kinematic equation:\n\\[ v^2 = (859.00 \\text{ cm/s})^2 + 2 \\times (2.452 \\text{ m/s}^2) \\times 264.65 \\text{ m} \\]\n\nFirst, convert \\( u \\) from cm/s to m/s:\n\\[ u = 859.00 \\text{ cm/s} = 859.00 \\times 0.01 \\text{ m/s} = 8.59 \\text{ m/s} \\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( u \\) and \\( a_1 \\) into the equation:\n\\[ v^2 = (8.59 \\text{ m/s})^2 + 2 \\times 2.452 \\text{ m/s}^2 \\times 264.65 \\text{ m} \\]\n\\[ v^2 = 73.7881 \\text{ m}^2/\\text{s}^2 + 1260.0712 \\text{ m}^2/\\text{s}^2 \\]\n\\[ v^2 = 1333.8593 \\text{ m}^2/\\text{s}^2 \\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[ v = \\sqrt{1333.8593} \\text{ m/s} \\]\n\\[ v \\approx 36.52 \\text{ m/s} \\]\n\nTherefore, the speed of the cart at the end of phase 1 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{36.52} \\]",
  "To determine the acceleration \\(a_1\\) of the crate during the pull, we start with Newton's second law, which states that the net force on an object is equal to the mass of the object times its acceleration. The net force in this case is the horizontal pull \\(F\\) minus the kinetic friction force \\(f_k\\).\n\nThe kinetic friction force \\(f_k\\) is given by:\n\\[ f_k = \\mu N \\]\nwhere \\(N\\) is the normal force, which is equal to the weight of the crate \\(mg\\). Therefore, the kinetic friction force is:\n\\[ f_k = \\mu mg \\]\n\nThe net force \\(F_{\\text{net}}\\) is then:\n\\[ F_{\\text{net}} = F - f_k = F - \\mu mg \\]\n\nUsing Newton's second law, we have:\n\\[ F_{\\text{net}} = ma_1 \\]\n\\[ F - \\mu mg = ma_1 \\]\n\\[ a_1 = \\frac{F - \\mu mg}{m} \\]\n\nGiven the values:\n\\[ F = 5.37 \\text{ N} \\]\n\\[ m = 2.19 \\text{ kg} \\]\n\\[ g = 9.81 \\text{ m/s}^2 \\]\n\\[ \\mu = 0.2 \\]\n\\[ F = 5.37 \\text{ N} \\]\n\nSubstituting these values into the equation for \\(a_1\\), we get:\n\\[ a_1 = \\frac{5.37 - 0.2 \\times 2.19 \\times 9.81}{2.19} \\]\n\\[ a_1 = \\frac{5.37 - 4.28}{2.19} \\]\n\\[ a_1 = \\frac{1.09}{2.19} \\]\n\\[ a_1 = 0.496 \\text{ m/s}^2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the acceleration of the crate during the pull is:\n\\[ \\boxed{0.496} \\]",
  "To find the kinetic energy of the block after the rough patch, \\( K_2 \\), we need to follow the energy transformations and work done by friction step by step.\n\n1. **Initial Energy at the Top of the ramp:**\n   The block starts from rest at height \\( h \\) with gravitational potential energy \\( U \\). At the bottom of the ramp, all of this potential energy is converted to kinetic energy.\n   \\[\n   U = mgh\n   \\]\n   Since the block is released from rest, the initial kinetic energy \\( K_1 \\) is zero. Therefore, the kinetic energy at the bottom of the ramp is:\n   \\[\n   K_1 = mgh\n   \\]\n\n2. **Work Done by Friction:**\n   The work done by friction \\( W_f \\) on the rough horizontal patch of length \\( L \\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   W_f = -\\mu mgL\n   \\]\n   The negative sign indicates that work done by friction is energy lost. The change in kinetic energy due to friction is:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta K = W_f = -\\mu mgL\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the kinetic energy after the rough patch \\( K_2 \\) is:\n   \\[\n   K_2 = K_1 + \\Delta K = mgh - \\mu mgL\n   \\]\n\n3. **Substitute the Given Values:**\n   We are given:\n   \\[\n   K_1 = 203.56 \\text{ J}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   W_f = 0.054238 \\text{ kJ} = 54.238 \\text{ J}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\mu = 0.05\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   L = 1 \\text{ m}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   m = 1 \\text{ kg} \\quad \\text{(since } K_1 = mgh \\text{ and } K_1 = 203.56 \\text{ J}, \\text{ we can find } m \\text{ if } h \\text{ is known, but we don't need } h \\text{ explicitly for } K_2\\text{)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   g = 9.81 \\text{ m/s}^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\mu mgL = 0.05 \\times 1 \\times 9.81 \\times 1 = 0.4905 \\text{ J}\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   K_2 = 203.56 \\text{ J} - 54.238 \\text{ J} = 149.322 \\text{ J}\n   \\]\n\nThe kinetic energy of the block after the rough patch is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{149.32}\n\\]"
 ],
